<description>
Move each source pixel toward the separator when it matches its aligned reference pixel, otherwise to the far edge.

Input structure:
- A single full separator line is part of a rectangular frame; the frame's
  extra cells mark the reference side.
- On the reference side, one row or column of colored pixels is aligned
  with one row or column of source pixels on the opposite side.
- Each side has its own dominant background color.

Rule:
- Compare each source pixel with the reference pixel on the same row or column.
- If their colors match, place the source pixel in the cell adjacent to the
  separator on the source side.
- If their colors differ, place the source pixel on the far outer edge along
  the same row or column.
- Erase the original source pixels to the source-side background; keep the
  frame and reference pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
The extra frame cells identify which side is the reference; equality with the
aligned reference pixel decides whether each source pixel moves inward or outward.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "single source pixels on the open side of the separator, aligned with reference pixels on the framed side",
  "path": "straight line perpendicular to the separator, staying on the same row or column",
  "dst": "adjacent to the separator when the source color equals the aligned reference color; otherwise the far outer edge on the source side",
  "trajectory": "paint only the final destination cell",
  "transform": "erase original source pixels to the source-side background; keep the separator frame and reference pixels unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  type: move_along_path
  grid: 8-16 rows x 8-16 cols
  colors:
    separator: one color forming the frame, distinct from both backgrounds
    backgrounds: each side has a dominant bg; may be same or different across sides
    tokens: 3-5 non-bg non-sep colors shared between ref and source pixels
  separator_frame:
    orientation: horizontal or vertical, not on outer edge
    line: one complete row/col of separator color spanning the grid
    u_frame: separator color also fills two perpendicular rails at the grid edges on the ref side only (no sep_color cells on source side)
    ref_side_depth: 2-8 cells; source_side_depth: 3-13 cells
  reference:
    placement: single row/col adjacent to separator (distance 1) on the framed side
    pixels: 5-7 single-cell tokens at distinct positions along the separator axis
    positions: avoid frame edges (first/last index); span >=80% of usable interior [observed]
  source:
    placement: single row/col on unframed side, distance 1 to (side_depth-1) from separator
    pixels: same count and same axis positions as reference tokens
    match_ratio: at least 1 match and 1 nonmatch; ~2 matches out of 5-7 [observed]
  output:
    unchanged: separator frame, reference pixels, all non-source-pixel cells
    rule: compare each source token color with aligned reference token color
    match: move source token to cell adjacent to separator on source side
    nonmatch: move source token to far outer edge on source side (same axis position)
    erasure: original source positions become source-side background
generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first, then invert
  steps:
    - Choose grid size, orientation, separator position, ref side, sep_color, two bg colors, and 3-5 token colors.
    - Build output scaffold with separator line and U-frame rails on ref side; fill both sides with their backgrounds.
    - Pick 5-7 interior axis positions spanning >=80% of usable range; place reference tokens on the adjacent-to-separator line inside the frame.
    - Choose a source line at distance 1 to (side_depth-1) from separator; designate ~2 positions as matches (same color as ref) and the rest as nonmatches.
    - In output, place match tokens adjacent to separator on source side, nonmatch tokens at far source edge.
    - Derive input by moving all output source tokens back to the chosen source line and erasing their output-only destinations to source bg.
```
